his is what european  leaders will be working for this  september and early in october in the in  the following meetings that they have  the difference would be that we will  control that we will manage that for the  moment we are simply reacting and as we  are reacting so is the market and we are  seeing that the market prices are  jumping unexpectedly  in a way that is much more volatile than  had it been at our own decision  and of course the euro itself follows  because it reflects a market sentiment  so do you think that european leaders  are doing enough to try and prepare for  a winter without russian gas i mean  chancellor schultz seems to think that  germany is in a good position to get  through or at least to scrape through  well indeed if that is the case then we  should take the decision much faster  than this  there would there will not be any  solution without a reduction  of consumption the question is by whom  at what pace  until when  we know that next winter is going to be  a difficult winter because we have to  decouple from russian  energy oil will happen by the end of the  year and gas well probably will happen  sooner than that but it will not happen  but from us it will happen  when the russians decide to do it that  is not good for the prices because it  makes it much more volatile  had the european union come more  decisively and said we are cutting it  off immediately or at the very  predictable  time path then all markets will have  organized themselves accordingly and  therefore the price will be a lot more  controlled the supply is what it is we  cannot do anything about that but what  we can do is control the timetable of it  can we read anything into putin's  approach here of cutting off supplies to  europe in terms of what the impact of  the sanctions have actually been for  russia's economy is it worse than the  countries letting on  well i think the impact of the sanctions  given the decisions that we've taken on  the russian economy will only be in the  medium term or the long term let's be  clear about this it would be  catastrophic for the russian economy but  we're not going to see it this time this  uh winter probably for another six  months or so simply because we are still  relying on energy on russian energy and  that of course puts the price up  um and it only benefits the revenues for  the russian economy and so that is the  bit that i think we still need to fix we  have to cut it off bite the bullet  and forbid  the russian economy for benefiting from  the huge incremental prices we've seen  on on energy this is the issue that  needs to be resolved the supply side we  have to cut it but also let's be clear  that the russian economy is going to  bake an enormous hit uh but with some  delay  okay maria de meza's from brugel thank  you very much for joining us here on dw  business
 

